- decided_at: 2026-09-23T00:41:50+00:00
  event: initial
  value: Design target, not implemented: trusted execution broker owns a Runner-lifetime manager and immutable per-run/per-agent scopes bound to one workspace and policy. All model or workspace-triggered subprocesses use the broker, including Git, builds, lint, background commands, and fused follow-ups. One process lifecycle supports foreground and background use, bounded output, scope-owned handles, and backend enforcement checks. Local backend first; isolated backend later, with no automatic fallback. Direct user shell execution has a separately constructed trusted scope. Preserve asynchronous child-agent lifetimes across interactive turns.
  reason: A bare command wrapper would leave lifecycle, policy, workspace mapping, and indirect subprocess escape paths scattered; sandboxing the entire controller would mix credentials and UI with untrusted execution. Scoped brokering provides a stable backend seam while preserving existing Runner-owned child lifetime. File tools and subprocesses must share a workspace view before isolation can be claimed.
- decided_at: 2026-09-23T01:36:51+00:00
  event: superseded-previous
  value: Implemented and validated: trusted execution broker in internal/execution owns a Runner-lifetime manager and immutable per-run/per-agent scopes bound to one workspace and policy. All model or workspace-triggered subprocesses (Git, builds, lint, background commands, fused follow-ups) route through it. Local backend only; RequireIsolation returns ErrUnsupported with no fallback. Direct user shell uses a separately constructed trusted scope. Regression suite in internal/execution plus an AST architecture test pinning exec.Command to the backend and the audited folder picker.
  reason: The design from the prior session is now the shipped structure: production exec.Command sites are confined to internal/execution/local.go and internal/folderpicker, and the boundary's claims (denial, workspace escape, isolation refusal, cancellation vs timeout, scope-owned handles, bounded output, stdin restriction) are enforced by tests rather than only documented.
